      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Given the expression $9x^2+3x$, we look for common factors to factor out from each term.\n\n1. Identify the common factors:\n   - For the coefficient of $x^2$, which is $9$, the factors are $1$, $3$, $9$, and $27$.\n   - For the coefficient of $x$, which is $3$, the factors are $1$ and $3$.\n   - The least common multiple (LCM) of the coefficients $9$ and $3$ is $9$.\n   - The greatest common factor (GCF) of the variable terms $x^2$ and $x$ is $x$.\n\n2. Factor out the GCF:\n   - The GCF is $3x$.\n\n3. Factor out $3x$ from each term:\n   \\[\n   9x^2 + 3x = 3x(3x + 1)\n   \\]\n\n4. Verify the factored form:\n   - Check if $3x(3x + 1)$ equals $9x^2 + 3x$:\n     \\[\n     3x(3x + 1) = 3x \\cdot 3x + 3x \\cdot 1 = 9x^2 + 3x\n     \\]\n\nThus, the completely factored form of the expression $9x^2 + 3x$ is \\boxed{3x(3x + 1)}.",
